WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:30.000
The news surrounding COVID-19 is pretty depressing. The stock markets are tanking, the price of oil collapsed. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention is warning that there could be a second more devastating round of infections ahead. Are you feeling anxious yet? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that looks at the connections between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills.
围绕新冠疫情的新闻令人相当沮丧。股市暴跌，油价崩盘。美国疾病控制与预防中心警告称，未来可能会出现第二波更具破坏性的感染。你感到焦虑了吗？欢迎收听《心理学谈话》，这是美国心理学会的旗舰播客，旨在探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯。

2
00:00:30.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Now that we're well into our second month of sheltering in place, people are maybe losing motivation, finding it hard to exercise and eat right and get enough sleep. And if you have kids, how are they doing? Are they keeping up with their schoolwork, or are you too stressed to find out? Joining us today is Dr. Cion Bylock, a psychologist and president of Barnard College in New York. Dr. Bylock is one of the foremost researchers into embodied cognition, which looks at the connections between our minds and our bodies. She's also studied anxiety, especially math, anxiety, and girls and women. And she is one of the world's leading experts on the brain science behind choking under pressure and the many brain and body factors influencing all types of performance.
现在我们已经进入居家隔离的第二个月，人们可能正在失去动力，觉得难以锻炼、难以健康饮食和保证充足睡眠。如果你有孩子，他们的情况如何？他们能跟上学校的功课吗，还是你因为压力太大而无暇顾及？今天加入我们的是心理学家、纽约巴纳德学院院长希恩·拜洛克博士。拜洛克博士是具身认知领域最重要的研究人员之一，该领域研究的是我们的思想与身体之间的联系。她还研究过焦虑，尤其是数学焦虑，以及女性和女孩的焦虑。她是全球公认的顶尖专家，研究压力下“发挥失常”背后的脑科学，以及影响各种表现的诸多大脑和身体因素。

3
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Welcome to speaking of psychology, Dr. Bylock. Thanks for having me. So in the interest of full disclosure, I have to say right up front that I graduated from Barnard with a degree in biology, but I will not tell you what year that was. That's fantastic. All right. That being said, I wanted to talk to you because of your varied and remarkable background and not because I went to Barnard. There are a lot of areas that we could talk about today.
欢迎来到《心理学谈话》，拜洛克博士。谢谢你的邀请。为了坦诚起见，我得先说明，我毕业于巴纳德学院，拥有生物学学位，但我不会告诉你那是哪一年。那太棒了。好了，话虽如此，我邀请你是因为你丰富而卓越的背景，而不是因为我曾就读于巴纳德。今天我们可以讨论很多领域。

4
00:01:37.000 --> 00:02:15.000
So I expect our conversation will be a little bit free-ranging if that's okay. Perfect. But I hope that we can relate a lot of what we talk about to the COVID-19 pandemic because that's what is on everyone's mind right now. So at APA, we talk a lot about the mind-body connection, how our mental health is inextricably linked to our physical health. research shows the connections are real and that they're strong. You've done a lot of research in the area of embodied cognition as it's called. Could you explain that in more detail and how might it be relevant to our lives right now? Yeah, I think oftentimes we think of the mind and body connected in a one-way street, so to speak.
所以我预计我们的谈话会比较自由，如果可以的话。没问题。但我希望我们能把讨论的很多内容与新冠疫情联系起来，因为这是目前每个人都在关心的问题。在美国心理学会，我们经常谈论身心联系，即我们的心理健康与身体健康是如何密不可分的。研究表明，这种联系是真实且强大的。你在所谓的“具身认知”领域做了大量研究。你能更详细地解释一下吗？它与我们当下的生活有何关联？是的，我认为我们往往认为身心联系是单向的，可以这么说。

5
00:02:15.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The mind sends signals to the body about how to act, what to do. But a lot of my research and the work behind embodied cognition suggests really that that that information flow runs both ways. So how you hold your body, whether you're sleeping enough, exercising has an impact on how you feel. And that's really important in this time, especially when many of us are indoors, not getting up as much, that we understand there is that strong connection and make it a priority in however way we can do it, whether it's inside or outside of social distancing, to get that body running because it helps us run our mind. So a lot of folks who are sheltering in place include children of all ages, many of whom are
大脑向身体发送关于如何行动、做什么的信号。但我的许多研究以及具身认知背后的工作表明，信息流实际上是双向的。因此，你的身体姿势、是否睡眠充足、是否锻炼，都会影响你的感受。在当前这个时期，这一点尤为重要，尤其是当我们许多人待在室内、活动减少时，我们必须理解这种强大的联系，并尽可能地将其作为优先事项，无论是在社交距离之内还是之外，都要让身体动起来，因为这有助于我们的大脑运作。所以，许多居家隔离的人包括各个年龄段的孩子，他们中有许多人

6
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:35.000
trying to keep up with their studies remotely. As an educator, do you have any advice for how parents can help their kids stay on top of their studies under these conditions? So I have some advice as a researcher and also as a mom to a nine-year-old who's doing, going through the same things. And I think, you know, the first thing to really remember as a parent is to give yourself a break, right? We can't hold ourselves. or our kids to the same standards we held ourselves outside the pandemic. And in many cases, it really doesn't matter. And the best example I like to give is with young kids.
正试图远程跟上学业。作为一名教育工作者，对于父母如何帮助孩子在这种情况下保持学业进度，你有什么建议吗？作为一名研究人员，同时也是一个正在经历同样事情的九岁孩子的母亲，我有一些建议。我认为，作为父母，首先要记住的是要让自己喘口气，对吧？我们不能用疫情之外的标准来要求自己或孩子。在许多情况下，这真的没那么重要。我喜欢给出的最好例子是关于幼儿的。

7
00:03:35.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Oftentimes parents are really obsessed with when their kid learns to read and whether their kid is the first reader in the class or early in the group. And all the research suggests that when a kid learns to read has no impact on later academic functioning. And so it's those kinds of ideas which really are important for us to remember. in terms of giving ourselves a break. So, for example, it's much more important that your young child feels good about math and has the viewpoint that he or she can learn math,
通常父母非常纠结于孩子什么时候学会阅读，以及他们的孩子是否是班上第一个学会阅读的，或者是否属于较早的一批。但所有研究都表明，孩子何时学会阅读对以后的学术表现没有影响。因此，这些观念对于我们记住“让自己喘口气”非常重要。例如，让你的幼儿对数学感觉良好，并认为他或她能够学习数学，这要重要得多，

8
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:38.000
that it's something you acquire with practice, then knows every fact for his or her age group. Our attitudes are really important right now, and I think that's something we can all focus on and we can all have control over. So that alludes to the research you've done into the connections between parents, math, anxiety, and that of their kids, and also just the parent connection to children's academic and social development. You talk a little more about those factors and how they might be playing into the current scenario of parents and kids sheltering in place.
这是一种通过练习就能获得的技能，而不是要求他们掌握同龄人所知的每一个事实。我们的态度在当下非常重要，我认为这是我们所有人都可以关注并掌控的事情。这提到了你所做的关于父母、数学焦虑及其对孩子影响的研究，以及父母与孩子学业和社会发展之间的联系。你能多谈谈这些因素，以及它们在当前父母和孩子居家隔离的情况下是如何发挥作用的吗？

9
00:04:38.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Yeah. So, first of all, it's just really important to remember that what parents do with their kids does matter. So I think oftentimes parents think that reading is something you might do at home. bedtime stories, for example. But math is the purview of school. I mean, you don't hear a lot of people doing bedtime math. That would be funny. And in fact, my research group has studied an app called bedtime math, which is free on iTunes and Android. And we've shown that when parents are able to engage with their kids in fun and interesting ways around math, their kids do better in school and math. And that's especially true for parents who are most anxious about math. So a lot of adults, especially in the U.S.
是的。首先，记住父母与孩子的互动确实很重要，这一点非常关键。我认为父母往往认为阅读是可以在家里做的事情，比如睡前故事。但数学是学校的范畴。我的意思是，你很少听到有人做“睡前数学”。那会很有趣。事实上，我的研究小组研究过一款名为“睡前数学”的应用程序，它在iTunes和安卓系统上都是免费的。我们已经证明，当父母能够以有趣和吸引人的方式与孩子一起参与数学活动时，他们的孩子在学校和数学方面的表现会更好。对于那些对数学最焦虑的父母来说，情况尤其如此。所以很多成年人，特别是在美国，

10
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:47.000
don't like math or afraid of math. You don't hear highly intelligent people walking around bragging that they're not readers, but you do hear them bragging that they're not good math people. And unfortunately, that does tend to seep in and affect our kids. And people who are anxious about math, parents tend to do less math with their kids. When they do help them with homework, they can disrupt it. And so really, right now, the most important thing,
不喜欢数学或害怕数学。你不会听到高智商的人到处吹嘘自己不爱阅读，但你确实会听到他们吹嘘自己不擅长数学。不幸的是，这种情绪往往会渗透并影响我们的孩子。对数学感到焦虑的父母往往会减少与孩子一起进行的数学活动。当他们帮助孩子做作业时，可能会产生干扰。所以现在，最重要的事情，

11
00:05:47.000 --> 00:06:09.000
I would say, especially for parents who are anxious, is to help your kid have a good attitude about math. Saying things like, oh, it's okay, I don't like math either, or I'm not a math person. Might be trying to make them feel good, but it's sending a signal that either you've got the math gene or you don't. But instead saying, hey, this is difficult. Let's struggle through this together,
我想说，特别是对于那些焦虑的父母，是帮助你的孩子对数学保持良好的态度。说些诸如“哦，没关系，我也不喜欢数学”或“我不是学数学的料”之类的话，可能是在试图让他们感觉好受些，但这传递了一个信号，即你要么有数学基因，要么没有。相反，应该说：“嘿，这很难。让我们一起努力克服它，”

12
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:34.000
showing that it's okay to make progress, but maybe not get the right answer. All those things are things we can model now for our kids that are so important. Some of your research has looked at how to reduce the performance gap, between lower income and higher income students, particularly in the sciences. Do you think that lower income kids are in danger of falling further behind as a result of the current pandemic? Are there strategies that you might recommend for parents to help their kids stay focused
展示出取得进步是可以的，即使没有得到正确答案也没关系。所有这些都是我们现在可以为孩子树立的榜样，它们非常重要。你的一些研究着眼于如何缩小低收入和高收入学生之间的表现差距，特别是在科学领域。你认为低收入家庭的孩子是否面临因当前疫情而进一步落后的危险？你有什么策略建议父母帮助孩子保持专注

13
00:06:34.000 --> 00:07:12.000
and not fall behind? I mean, I certainly think that when we're in a state like this, it oftentimes augments socioeconomic divides rather than shrinks them, unfortunately. And so it is really important to think about how we can support everyone. At Barnard, for example, we went to a mandatory pass, fail for all of our classes, for all of our students for the remainder of the semester when we went online. And the reason we did that is because we knew that we could somewhat control students living environments when they were on campus, but we couldn't when they went home.
而不掉队吗？我的意思是，我当然认为当我们处于这种状态时，不幸的是，它往往会加剧而不是缩小社会经济差距。因此，思考我们如何支持每个人确实非常重要。例如，在巴纳德学院，当我们转为线上教学时，我们对所有学生在剩余学期内的所有课程实行了强制性的“通过/不通过”评分制。我们这样做的原因是，我们知道当学生在校园时，我们可以控制他们的生活环境，但当他们回家后，我们就无法控制了。

14
00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:56.000
And some kids were going to their parents' second home, and some kids were going home to a family where they were going to have to help support what was going on. And we really did this for inequality reason. We didn't want some students to suffer just because they didn't have the time or the means to be able to focus in the right way. And so this is something especially important to think about with parents and their kids as well. Many lower income families, their parents are essential workers and working on the front lines are, it's stressful, right? So anything that we can think of to help make these activities fun for our kids, to even ask them what they're doing in school to at least engage in conversations, I'd say anything right now goes a long way.
有些孩子回到了父母的第二个家，而有些孩子回到了需要他们帮忙维持生计的家庭。我们这样做确实是出于不平等的考虑。我们不希望有些学生仅仅因为没有时间或条件以正确的方式专注学习而受到影响。因此，在考虑父母和孩子的问题时，这一点也特别重要。许多低收入家庭的父母是必要工作人员，在第一线工作，这很有压力，对吧？所以，任何能让我们想到让这些活动对孩子来说变得有趣的方法，甚至只是问问他们在学校做什么，至少进行一些对话，我认为现在任何一点努力都会大有帮助。

15
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:48.000
So since much of your research has focused on how our physical surroundings shape, how we feel, think, and behave, how do you think sheltering in place might affect our minds and our bodies? Well, we know that, you know, a lack of movement, changing surroundings can affect our ability to be creative. So, for example, there's psychological research showing that when you get up and walk away from a problem, you're often likely to gain that aha moment. It's not necessarily when you're banging your head against the wall. And it's much easier to do that when you're in an office and you get up to go to the water cooler or when you commute to and from work. And so now I would say it's important for us to create those strategies, right? How can we set a schedule or we do have time to get away from our computer? At what time at night do we turn off our phone and computer?
既然你的许多研究都集中在我们的物理环境如何塑造我们的感受、思维和行为上，你认为居家隔离可能会如何影响我们的思想和身体？嗯，我们知道，缺乏运动、环境的变化会影响我们的创造力。例如，有心理学研究表明，当你起身离开问题时，你往往更有可能获得“顿悟”时刻。这不一定是在你撞墙的时候。当你身处办公室，起身去饮水机旁，或者上下班通勤时，这样做要容易得多。所以现在，我认为重要的是我们要制定这些策略，对吧？我们如何设定时间表，让我们有时间离开电脑？晚上几点关掉手机和电脑？

16
00:08:48.000 --> 00:09:32.000
And how do we help create surroundings that can relax and infigrate and energize us? And in opposition to what we used to know is normal daily life, I think we have to do a little more work to create that now. So we know the COVID-19 pandemic is affecting higher education, just like it is other workplaces and institutions. I wanted to talk a little more about what's happening at Barnard, which is relatively small, about 2,400 students, right? Yeah. Also, maybe what's happening at the larger Columbia University world and beyond. What is happening to the students and the faculty right now? Well, Barnard is really special because it's a small institution-focused.
我们如何帮助创造能够放松、激发活力和赋予我们能量的环境？与我们过去认为的正常日常生活相比，我认为我们现在必须多做一点工作来创造这种环境。我们知道新冠疫情正在影响高等教育，就像它影响其他工作场所和机构一样。我想多谈谈巴纳德学院的情况，它相对较小，大约有2400名学生，对吧？是的。还有，哥伦比亚大学及其他地方正在发生什么？学生和教职员工现在怎么样了？嗯，巴纳德很特别，因为它是一个专注于

17
00:09:32.000 --> 00:10:20.000
on empowering women. So you get this small, fantastic liberal arts education, but under the umbrella of the larger Columbia University. So I always talk about it as the best of all worlds. And we're linked in a lot of ways. So for example, many students at Columbia come over to Barnard to take classes every day and vice versa. And so there's so many ways that we're interconnected. And it's been really interesting to watch the whole university system and Barnard really make a quick move online. I mean, we did this in just a few weeks, and I've been so proud of our faculty and our students. I've been doing weekly coffee chats with about 10 of our students online each week, and they talk about the faculty and how important it is for them to have the classes and stay
赋权女性的小型机构。所以你得到的是这种小型、出色的文科教育，但处于哥伦比亚大学这个更大的保护伞下。所以我总是说它是两全其美的。我们在很多方面都是相连的。例如，许多哥伦比亚大学的学生每天都到巴纳德上课，反之亦然。所以我们以多种方式相互关联。看着整个大学系统和巴纳德迅速转向线上教学，这非常有趣。我的意思是，我们在短短几周内就完成了这一点，我为我们的教职员工和学生感到非常自豪。我每周都会与大约10名学生进行在线咖啡聊天，他们谈论教职员工，谈论拥有课程并保持

18
